{"name":"Herb Cornucopia","hex":"#6e7357","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"herb","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant whose stem is not woody and does not persist beyond each growing season.","Grass; herbage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/herb"},{"term":"cornucopia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A goat's horn endlessly overflowing with fruit, flowers and grain; or full of whatever its owner wanted: or, an image of a such a horn, either in two or three dimensions.","A hollow horn- or cone-shaped object, filled with edible or useful things."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cornucopia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Herb","blocks":[{"text":"Herbs are a widely distributed and widespread group of plants, excluding vegetables, with savory or aromatic properties that are used for flavoring and garnishing food, for medicinal purposes, or for fragrances. Culinary use typically distinguishes herbs from spices. Herbs generally refers to the leafy green or flowering parts of a plant, while spices are usually dried and produced from other parts of the plant, including seeds, bark, roots and fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Garden_of_herbs.jpg/330px-Garden_of_herbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herb","wikidata":"Q207123"},{"title":"Cornucopia","blocks":[{"text":"In classical antiquity, the cornucopia, also called the horn of plenty, is a symbol of abundance and nourishment, commonly a large horn-shaped container overflowing with produce, flowers, or nuts. In Greek, it was called the horn of Amalthea, after Amalthea, a nurse of Zeus, who is often part of stories of the horn's origin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Cornucopia_Palermo.jpg/330px-Cornucopia_Palermo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","li